-- Intent: RE-SEED cloud exercise_library from bundled assets/data/exercise_library.json (914 exercises, library v15, Batch C1): UPSERTS the 629 new rows (source 'exercise-gif-set') beside the 285 existing ones, whose values are re-written unchanged, and repeats the 7 idempotent Batch A retire UPDATEs (those rows are already inactive). No row is deactivated by this file that is not already inactive.
-- Destructive?: yes   -- UPSERT REWRITES ROWS (supabase/migrations/CLAUDE.md): ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE re-writes every column of the 285 existing rows (with their current values) and inserts 629 new ones. No DELETE, DROP, TRUNCATE or constraint change.
-- Rollback strategy: inline   -- UPDATE exercise_library SET is_active = false WHERE source = 'exercise-gif-set';  (selects exactly the 629 new rows; commented block at the end of this file). NOT a DELETE: promotions and logs may point at a row once users see it.
-- Linked diagnose-doc: 2026-10-26-library-batch-c1-c1d7a4

-- Source: 914 exercises from assets/data/exercise_library.json.
-- Deterministic UUIDs v5 from (namespace, "exercise_library|<json_id>") so re-runs are idempotent.
-- See scripts/seed_exercise_library.js for regeneration.
--
-- Schema mapping notes:
--   * movement_pattern + exercise_type are TEXT in DB but arrays in JSON — flatten to first element.
--   * name_aliases comes from the JSON since library v12 (NULL on a row without one).
--   * DB columns not in JSON (instructions, alternative_ids, regression_id,
--     progression_id, default_duration_secs) are intentionally NULL on first apply.
--   * JSON-only fields (tempo, met_value, cal_per_set_est, breathing_cue, warmup_protocol,
--     pro_tip, image_*, gif_url, equipment_tier, cns_demand, target_focus, priority_tier,
--     rep_range, is_foundational, is_bilateral, standard_swap, indian_alternative,
--     injury_contraindications, core_levels, discipline, gym_availability, requires_coaching,
--     high_impact, variant_family and the ladder keys) are not surfaced in cloud schema yet —
--     they live in the bundled Hive box. Add columns + a follow-up backfill migration when a
--     reader needs them.

